      "preview_head": "but also an intermediate pretreatment step to convert solid biomass into a higher energy content transportable liquid for subsequent processing for heat, power, biofuels, and chemicals (Bridgwater, [@",
      "preview_tail": "[@B18]). The fast pyrolysis process is shown in Figure 1. ![](../figs/fig1.png) Figure 1. The fast pyrolysis process (Pant et al., [@B18]) The pyrolysis products are usually classified into three majo",
      "full_text": "but also an intermediate pretreatment step to convert solid biomass into a higher energy content transportable liquid for subsequent processing for heat, power, biofuels, and chemicals (Bridgwater, [@B3]). But, the bio-oil obtained by single-step pyrolysis is composed of much complex mixture of oxygenated hydrocarbons with an appreciable proportion of water (Bridgwater, [@B3]), which leads to a lower calorific value, unstable burning and difficult to refine. The stepwise pyrolysis could improve the utilization of bio-oil according to different thermal decomposition behavior of celluolitic materials (Zhang et al., [@B23]). The biomass was firstly heated in the first step and then rapidly cooled down to keep the thermal decomposition products in a solid state (Zhang et al., [@B23]). After that, the product of the first step was pyrolyzed at high temperature in the second step. Thus, the bio-oil obtained from the second step has better quality with less water content and higher calorific value (Zhang et al., [@B23]). This paper aims to review the current status of fast pyrolysis technology for bio-oil production and its application in China. Bio-oil production by fast pyrolysis ------------------------------------ Fast pyrolysis is a thermo-chemical process that involves rapid heating of biomass to 400~600\u00b0C with limited oxygen supply, resulting in a rapid devolatilization (Pant et al., [@B18]). The high heating rate and the low oxygen content in the system lead to the production of bio-oil, which has high energy density and can be used as fuel directly or as an intermediate product for further upgrading (Pant et al., [@B18]). The fast pyrolysis process is shown in Figure 1. ![](../figs/fig1.png) Figure 1. The fast pyrolysis process (Pant et al., [@B18]) The pyrolysis products are usually classified into three majo",
      "avg_prediction": 0.5036,
